</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Marco Grangetto
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Turin, Italy
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Effective concept unlearning in text-to-image diffusion models requires precise localization of concept representations within the model’s latent space. While sparse autoencoders successfully reduce neuron polysemanticity (i.e., multiple concepts per neuron) compared to the original network, individual concept representations can still be distributed across multiple latent features, requiring extensive search procedures for concept unlearning. We introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span>, a supervised sparse autoencoder training method that promotes one-to-one concept-neuron mappings through systematic concept labeling, mitigating feature splitting and promoting feature centralization.
Our approach learns specialized neurons with significantly stronger concept associations compared to unsupervised baselines. The only computational overhead introduced by <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> is limited to cross-entropy computation during training. At inference time, this interpretable representation reduces hyperparameter search by 96.67% with respect to current approaches. On the UnlearnCanvas benchmark, <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> achieves an 9.22% improvement over the state-of-the-art. In sequential unlearning tasks, we demonstrate superior scalability with a 28.4% improvement in unlearning accuracy for 9-object removal.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Text-to-image diffusion models have achieved remarkable success in generating high-quality images from textual descriptions, with applications across diverse domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. However, they can also produce harmful, inappropriate, or copyrighted content when given specific prompts, raising serious safety concerns. This has spurred growing interest in machine unlearning methods, which aim to selectively remove undesired concepts from trained models while preserving their generative abilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.21379v1/teaser_saemnesia_v2.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/645;" width="685" height="645" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">SAEmnesia<span id="S1.F1.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> enables precise concept-level manipulation<span id="S1.F1.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: each latent activates for a single concept (<span id="S1.F1.10.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">monosemanticity</span>), and each concept is embedded in a single latent (<span id="S1.F1.10.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">feature centralization</span>). So, to erase a target concept, we only need to steer a single latent. The removed concepts correctly disappear in the diagonal images (<em id="S1.F1.10.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Architectures</em>, <em id="S1.F1.10.1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Bears</em>, <em id="S1.F1.10.1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Flames</em>), while remaining present in the non-diagonal ones, thereby preserving the fidelity and diversity of unrelated content.</span></span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A core challenge in concept unlearning is identifying where and how concepts are represented inside these models, especially when the number of trainable parameters is very high. Additionally, a single neuron can be active for many different unrelated concepts. This phenomenon is known as <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">polysemanticity</span>, making interpretability even more challenging. Mechanistic interpretability (MI) seek to understand the internal workings of neural networks by analyzing their components and representations. Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) provide a particularly effective MI tool by decomposing model activations into sparse and interpretable concept-level features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Note that, in this work, we adopt the notion
of features as the fundamental units of neural network representations that cannot be further decomposed into simpler independent factors, as defined by  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bereska and Gavves [4]</a></cite>. Neural networks can capture natural abstractions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> through their learned
features, which serve as building blocks of their internal representations, aiming to capture the concepts
underlying the data. For simplicity, we use the terms “concepts” and “features” interchangeably, as well as “neurons” and “latents”. SAEs aim to learn <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">monosemantic</span> latents, meaning that they activate almost exclusively for a specific concept. On the other hand, to improve models’ interpretability even further, individual concepts should not be spread across many latents, aiming for a one-to-one mapping. Yet, in practice, multiple latents often respond to the same concept, this phenomenon is also known as <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">feature splitting</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. This distributed nature poses two key challenges: (i) it necessitates exhaustive searches across many latent combinations to find the right subset to modify, making unlearning computationally expensive; and (ii) overlapping latents blur concept boundaries, so interventions risk unintended side effects on related concepts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome feature splitting, we introduce <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</em>, that enriches the SAE training framework enforcing one-to-one mappings between concepts and latents through supervised labeling. Therefore, our method achieves <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">feature centralization</span>, localizing each concept into a single latent feature and thereby preventing splitting across multiple latents. This binding simplifies mechanistic unlearning, thanks to a precise single-latent intervention. Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates the effect of <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> for concept removal in generative models, demonstrating the possibility of precise control over what the model generates, while retaining overall quality and diversity. On the UnlearnCanvas benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> achieves an 9.22% improvement over the state-of-the-art mechanistic approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. In sequential unlearning tasks, we demonstrate superior scalability with a 28.4% improvement in unlearning accuracy for 9-object removal. Furthermore, at inference time, this interpretable representation reduces hyperparameter search by 96.67%.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Machine Unlearning in Diffusion Models.</span> The term and problem statement for machine unlearning was first introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao and Yang [6]</a></cite>, who transformed neural network models through additional simple layers into formats where output is a summation of independent features, allowing unlearning by blocking selected summation weights or nodes. However, recent works focusing on unlearning for diffusion models typically employ fine-tuning approaches to unlearn specific concepts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">EDiff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> formulates this problem as bi-level optimization, while ESD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> leverages negative classifier-free guidance for concept removal. FMN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> introduces a re-steering loss applied only to attention layers, and SalUn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> and SHS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> select parameters to adapt through saliency maps or connection sensitivity. SA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> replaces unwanted data distribution with surrogate distributions, with an extension to selected anchor concepts in CA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. SPM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> takes a different approach, using small linear adapters added after each linear and convolutional layer to directly block unwanted content propagation. In contrast to these fine-tuning approaches, our work leverages SAEs to achieve unlearning through interpretable feature manipulation during inference, without modifying the base model weights and providing full transparency into which specific features are being targeted for removal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By contrast, methods that do not rely on fine-tuning include SEOT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, which eliminates unwanted content from text embeddings, and UCE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, which adapts cross-attention weights using closed-form solutions. Unlike these approaches that modify prompt embeddings or alter model weights, our method operates through interpretable feature interventions on internal activations during inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond individual methods, recent surveys synthesize objectives, taxonomies, and evaluation protocols for generative model unlearning, offering broader context for method design and assessment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, recent analyses highlight instability and concept resurgence after unlearning (such as revival under subsequent fine-tuning or adversarial prompting) reinforcing the need for interpretable and stable interventions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SAE Background.</span> Our objective is to enable effective concept unlearning in diffusion models by selectively removing unwanted concepts while preserving generative quality. To achieve this, we decompose the high-dimensional, entangled activations from Stable Diffusion (SD) into sparse, interpretable feature directions that correspond to meaningful visual concepts. SAEs serve as the key tool for this decomposition, enabling us to map individual neurons to specific semantic concepts and subsequently intervene on them for targeted unlearning. A standard single-layer ReLU SAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> operates on <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional activation vectors. Let <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the input activation vector and <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> be the latent dimension, typically set to <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> multiplied by a positive expansion factor. The encoder and decoder are defined as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathbf{v}&amp;=\text{ReLU}(W_{enc}(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{b}_{pre})+\mathbf{b}_{enc})\\
\hat{\mathbf{x}}&amp;=W_{dec}\mathbf{v}+\mathbf{b}_{pre},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi>𝐯</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>ReLU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathbf{v}&amp;=\text{ReLU}(W_{enc}(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{b}_{pre})+\mathbf{b}_{enc})\\
\hat{\mathbf{x}}&amp;=W_{dec}\mathbf{v}+\mathbf{b}_{pre},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{enc}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{enc}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{dec}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{dec}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> are the encoder and decoder weight matrices respectively, and <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{pre}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{pre}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{enc}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{enc}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable bias terms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TopK SAEs.</span> In our work, we employ TopK SAEs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> that provide enhanced sparsity control. The TopK activation function identifies the <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> largest pre-activation values and sets all others to zero, ensuring sparsity while preserving the most significant features. Following recent advances, we adopt this approach where the encoder becomes:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathbf{z}&amp;=\text{TopK}(\mathbf{v}),\\
\hat{\mathbf{x}}&amp;=W_{dec}\mathbf{z}+\mathbf{b}_{pre},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi>𝐳</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>TopK</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathbf{z}&amp;=\text{TopK}(\mathbf{v}),\\
\hat{\mathbf{x}}&amp;=W_{dec}\mathbf{z}+\mathbf{b}_{pre},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">while the decoder remains unchanged. Here, <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the pre-TopK activations, while <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the post-TopK sparse activations. This modification retains only the <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> largest latent activations for each vector <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, providing better control over sparsity compared to standard ReLU activations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Objective.</span> The TopK SAE loss combines reconstruction error with an auxiliary loss to prevent dead latents:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{unsupSAE}=||\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}||_{2}^{2}+\alpha\mathcal{L}_{aux}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{unsupSAE}=||\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}||_{2}^{2}+\alpha\mathcal{L}_{aux},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}||_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}||_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the reconstruction error and <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{aux}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{aux}</annotation></semantics></math> is an auxiliary loss using only the largest <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{aux}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{aux}</annotation></semantics></math> feature activations that have not fired on a large number of training samples (so-called dead latents). The auxiliary loss prevents dead latents from occurring and is scaled by coefficient <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interpretability of Vision Models.</span> SAEs have recently gained traction as a tool for uncovering human-interpretable structure in high-dimensional representations. Early applications focused on discriminative models, such as interpreting features in CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> or traditional classifiers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. More recent work has extended SAEs to vision–language settings, enabling tasks such as hallucination mitigation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and interpretable report generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Across these domains, SAEs provide explicit concept–neuron mappings that move beyond post-hoc explanations and allow more precise control over learned representations. Unlike previous work on discriminative models, our approach uses SAEs with diffusion models to directly identify and control specific concepts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Within diffusion models, interpretability research has primarily examined how semantic information propagates through the architecture. Studies have identified meaningful directions in UNet bottlenecks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, analyzed cross-attention to link prompts with spatial activations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, and even used intermediate text encoder states for generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>. More recent work has highlighted interpretable feature detectors in SDXL-Turbo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> and timestep-specific visual features in unconditional sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. While these advances shed light on model internals, they generally stop short of enabling targeted interventions. Our approach yields sparse representations that support both interpretation and controllable unlearning of specific concepts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SAE-Based Unlearning.</span> Recent work has explored applying SAEs to diffusion models for concept manipulation. These approaches differ from ours in their intervention points and training strategies. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim and Ghadiyaram [22]</a></cite> introduced Concept Steerers, training SAEs on text embedding representations to identify concept-specific directions before cross-attention processing. While their approach achieves effective concept manipulation, working only on text encoders can lead to suboptimal results, especially when facing adversarial attacks that exploit deeper model representations. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cywiński and Deja [9]</a></cite> introduced SAeUron, a post-cross-attention approach using SAEs trained on diffusion model activations in an unsupervised manner. While SAeUron achieves state-of-the-art performance on UnlearnCanvas, its unsupervised training creates weak concept-latent associations. This means that concepts are still represented across multiple neurons. This distributed representation requires computationally expensive feature threshold searches to identify which combination of latent features must be modified to unlearn each concept.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach addresses these limitations through supervised training that enforces strong one-to-one concept-latent relationships, enabling precise single-neuron interventions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.21379v1/pipeline_saemnesia.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/143;" width="685" height="143" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">SAEmnesia<span id="S3.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> comprises two phases: (i) establishing sparse representations via standard unsupervised SAE training, (ii) applying supervised losses to strengthen specific concept-neuron associations.
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike unsupervised SAE training methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> that require post-hoc discovery of concept-relevant features, our supervised approach directly enforces concept-latent assignments during training to achieve stronger one-to-one mappings. As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> consists of two phases: (i) establishing sparse representations through standard unsupervised SAE training, (ii) applying supervised losses to strengthen specific concept-neuron associations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia<span id="S3.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> for Diffusion Models.</span></span>
We apply <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> to diffusion models by training on activations extracted from every timestep <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> of the denoising diffusion process. These activations are obtained from the cross-attention blocks of the diffusion model and form feature maps. Each feature map extracted at timestep <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is a spatially structured tensor of shape <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times w\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times w\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> denote the height and width of the feature map, and <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimensionality of each feature vector. Each spatial position within the feature map corresponds to a patch in the input image. As a single SAE training sample, we consider an individual <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional feature vector <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, disregarding the information about its spatial position. Therefore, from each feature map, we obtain <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\times w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\times w</annotation></semantics></math> training samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Concept-Latent Assignment.</span> This assignment is required in the second phase, in which we introduce supervised training that directly assigns concepts to specific latents. To determine which latent should be assigned to each concept during the supervised phase and to validate the quality of these assignments after training, we utilize the score function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, defined in Equation <a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, measuring feature-concept correspondence. Given a dataset of activations <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=D_{c}\cup D_{\neg c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=D_{c}\cup D_{\neg c}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> contains data of the target concept <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\neg c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\neg c}</annotation></semantics></math> does not, the score function is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\text{score}(i,t,c,D)&amp;=\frac{\mu(i,t,D_{c})}{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\mu(j,t,D_{c})+\delta}\\
&amp;\quad-\frac{\mu(i,t,D_{\neg c})}{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\mu(j,t,D_{\neg c})+\delta}\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mtext>score</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\text{score}(i,t,c,D)&amp;=\frac{\mu(i,t,D_{c})}{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\mu(j,t,D_{c})+\delta}\\
&amp;\quad-\frac{\mu(i,t,D_{\neg c})}{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\mu(j,t,D_{\neg c})+\delta}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> prevents division by zero and <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(i,t,D)=\frac{1}{|D|}\sum_{x\in D}z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(i,t,D)=\frac{1}{|D|}\sum_{x\in D}z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the average activation of the <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th feature on activations from timestep <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (we omit <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for simplicity). Features with high scores exhibit strong activation for concept <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> while remaining weakly activated for other concepts.
Formally, in order to achieve feature centralization, for a given concept <math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, the score function score<math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,t,c,D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,t,c,D)</annotation></semantics></math> achieves a high value for a single latent index <math id="S3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, while remaining low for all other indices <math id="S3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq i</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{score}(i,t,c,D)\gg\text{score}(j,t,c,D),\quad\forall j\neq i." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>score</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≫</mo><mrow><mtext>score</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{score}(i,t,c,D)\gg\text{score}(j,t,c,D),\quad\forall j\neq i.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, for each concept <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, we assign it to the latent index <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> with the highest score, creating a mapping <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:\mathcal{C}\rightarrow\{1,2,...,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:\mathcal{C}\rightarrow\{1,2,...,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of concepts to unlearn. For training samples containing multiple concepts, we assign multiple target indices corresponding to each present concept.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia<span id="S3.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> Loss Function.</span></span> We employ a composite loss function designed to maintain SAE reconstruction capabilities while strengthening concept-latent associations:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{{SAEmnesia}}}=\mathcal{L}_{unsupSAE}+\beta\mathcal{L}_{supSAE}+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{L1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{{SAEmnesia}}}=\mathcal{L}_{unsupSAE}+\beta\mathcal{L}_{supSAE}+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{L1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Where <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{unsupSAE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{unsupSAE}</annotation></semantics></math> is the loss as defined in Equation (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{L1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{L1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the sparsity regularization term, and <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{supSAE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{supSAE}</annotation></semantics></math> is the supervised loss that enforces the desired concept-latent relationships.
<math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{supSAE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{supSAE}</annotation></semantics></math> consist of our Concept Assignment (CA) loss with a weighted additional Orthogonality Constraint (OC) loss:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{supSAE}=\mathcal{L}_{CA}+\gamma\mathcal{L}_{OC}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{supSAE}=\mathcal{L}_{CA}+\gamma\mathcal{L}_{OC}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Concept Assignment Loss.</span> Unlike traditional approaches that only apply reconstruction loss globally across all latents, our method requires additional supervision to strengthen concept-latent bonds that enable one-to-one mappings. We employ the CA loss that is computed exclusively for latents that are assigned to concepts present in each training sample, encouraging these specific latents to activate strongly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For each training sample <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we identify the set of target positions <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{j:" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{j:</annotation></semantics></math> latent <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is assigned to a concept present in sample <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The CA loss measures how much these assigned latents activate when their corresponding concepts are present:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{\text{CA}}&amp;=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}|}\sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{T}}\Big[-\log(\sigma(v^{i}_{j}))\Big],\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CA</mtext></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{\text{CA}}&amp;=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}|}\sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{T}}\Big[-\log(\sigma(v^{i}_{j}))\Big],\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the pre-activation (logit) for latent <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in sample <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid function. This loss directly penalizes assigned latents that fail to achieve strong activation when their concepts are present, while completely ignoring latents not assigned to concepts in each sample.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Orthogonality Constraint.</span> A critical challenge in concept unlearning is ensuring that removing one concept (e.g., a specific object) does not inadvertently affect related but distinct concepts (e.g., artistic styles). This problem arises when latents representing different concepts become correlated, leading to entangled representations where modifications to one concept cascade into unintended changes in others. For instance, if a latent assigned to ”cars” becomes correlated with a latent representing ”impressionist style,” then unlearning cars might also diminish the model’s ability to generate impressionist artwork.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">To address this entanglement issue, we introduce an across-batch orthogonality constraint that explicitly decorrelates the activation patterns of object and style latents:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{OC}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{O}|\cdot|\mathcal{S}|}\sum_{o\in\mathcal{O}}\sum_{s\in\mathcal{S}}\rho^{2}(a_{o},a_{s})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{OC}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{O}|\cdot|\mathcal{S}|}\sum_{o\in\mathcal{O}}\sum_{s\in\mathcal{S}}\rho^{2}(a_{o},a_{s}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the sets of assigned object and style latent indices, <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{o}=[v^{1}_{o},v^{2}_{o},\ldots,v^{B}_{o}]^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>o</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>B</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{o}=[v^{1}_{o},v^{2}_{o},\ldots,v^{B}_{o}]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the pre-TopK activation vector across the batch for latent labeled with object <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> (similarly for <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>), and <math id="S3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(a_{o},a_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(a_{o},a_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> is the Pearson correlation coefficient. This constraint minimizes correlations between activation patterns of different latents across the batch, ensuring the information of different concepts remains separated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sparsity Regularization.</span> To encourage sparse activations in the latent representation, we incorporate an L1 regularization term that penalizes the magnitude of latent activations:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{L1}=\frac{1}{|N|}\sum_{j=1}^{|N|}\|v_{j}\|_{1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{L1}=\frac{1}{|N|}\sum_{j=1}^{|N|}\|v_{j}\|_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p10.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|N|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|N|</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of latents and <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the activation for latent <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. This sparsity constraint promotes the emergence of interpretable features by encouraging most latents to remain inactive for any given input, thereby improving the disentanglement of learned representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feature Centralization.</span> As defined in Equation <a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, feature centralization occurs when only one feature achieves a high score for a specific concept. However, computing the score function requires the entire dataset, making it impractical during training. Instead, we use <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{supSAE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{supSAE}</annotation></semantics></math> as a proxy to achieve feature centralization. <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{CA}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{CA}</annotation></semantics></math> directly enforces that assigned latents activate strongly for their target concepts, while <math id="S3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{L1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{L1}</annotation></semantics></math> maintains sparsity across all latents. This combination concentrates a concept’s information in the designated latent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference and Concept Unlearning.</span> To unlearn a concept <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, we first identify the single top SAE feature associated with <math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and compute its average activation on a validation dataset <math id="S3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{c}:=\{i|i\in\text{Top-}1(\{\text{score}(i,t,c,D)\})\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>Top-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>score</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{c}:=\{i|i\in\text{Top-}1(\{\text{score}(i,t,c,D)\})\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(i):=\mu(i,t,D),\forall i\in F_{c}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(i):=\mu(i,t,D),\forall i\in F_{c}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p12.2" class="ltx_p">Note that this step is similar to the one proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cywiński and Deja [9]</a></cite>; however, <span id="S3.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> only needs a single latent to erase each concept.
The activation of the selected feature <math id="S3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is multiplied by a negative value <math id="S3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{c}&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{c}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> normalized by the average activation on concept samples <math id="S3.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(i,t,D_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(i,t,D_{c})</annotation></semantics></math>. This removes the influence of the targeted concept on the activation vector <math id="S3.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each <math id="S3.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th latent feature activation is modified as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}=\begin{cases}\gamma_{c}\mu(i,t,D_{c})z_{i},&amp;\text{if }i\in F_{c}\land z_{i}&gt;\mu(i,t,D)\\
z_{i},&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}=\begin{cases}\gamma_{c}\mu(i,t,D_{c})z_{i},&amp;\text{if }i\in F_{c}\land z_{i}&gt;\mu(i,t,D)\\
z_{i},&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p12.3" class="ltx_p">The condition <math id="S3.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}&gt;\mu(i,t,D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}&gt;\mu(i,t,D)</annotation></semantics></math> prevents random feature ablation when scores are low.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct comprehensive experiments to evaluate <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> across multiple dimensions: unlearning effectiveness, generation quality, concept separation, performance robustness, incremental unlearning capabilities, and adversarial resilience. Note that our focus here is on erasing objects, since it’s way more challenging that erasing styles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. This comprehensive evaluation framework allows us to demonstrate not only the effectiveness of our approach in removing unwanted concepts but also its practical applicability, robustness, and scalability in real-world deployment scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset.</span>
We extract internal activations from cross-attention blocks of SD1.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> from UnlearnCanvas Benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>. Our approach differs from unsupervised methods in the data collection methodology through systematic concept labeling during data generation. Rather than requiring post-hoc concept discovery, we construct labeled training data by systematically generating activations from structured prompts with known concept compositions. For each object class <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., <em id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Bears</em>, <em id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cats</em>) and style <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., <em id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Impressionism</em>, <em id="S4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cubism</em>), we generate 80 prompts of the form “a [<em id="S4.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">object</em>] in [<em id="S4.p2.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">style</em>] style” and collect feature maps from selected U-Net cross-attention blocks across all 50 denoising timesteps during text-conditioned generation. Each activation is directly labeled with its corresponding object and style based on the prompt structure, creating explicit concept-activation pairs. The employed objects and styles are taken from the UnlearnCanvas benchmark, which consists of 20 different objects and 50 different styles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We focus our analysis on block <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">up.1.1</span> for object-related features, as this block has been empirically demonstrated to specialize in generating specific visual aspects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. This controlled labeling strategy provides clean supervision signals that enable direct concept-neuron mapping during SAE training, contrasting with unsupervised approaches that must discover concept representations through post-hoc analysis.
For evaluation, we employed the same setting of SAeUron <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SAE Model.</span> Our best-performing method starts with a pre-trained unsupervised SAE and fine-tunes it using CA loss (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) combined with the orthogonality constraint (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) to enforce concept separation. The weights chosen for the loss function components and additional model setups can be found in the Appendix <a href="#S7.SS10" title="7.10 Lambda Coefficient Analysis for SAEmnesia Loss ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.10</span></a>, <a href="#S7.SS5" title="7.5 Global Cross-Entropy ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.5</span></a>, <a href="#S7.SS6" title="7.6 Post-TopK Loss Analysis ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a> and <a href="#S7.SS7" title="7.7 Exploratory Variants ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Metrics.</span> We employ a comprehensive evaluation framework that assesses multiple aspects of unlearning performance and model behavior. Our primary evaluation uses the UnlearnCanvas benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> with Vision Transformer-based classifiers to measure three key metrics: Unlearning Accuracy (UA), which quantifies the proportion of samples from target concept prompts that are misclassified (i.e., successful unlearning); In-domain Retain Accuracy (IRA), measuring classification accuracy on retained concepts within the same domain; and Cross-domain Retain Accuracy (CRA), assessing accuracy on concepts from different domains such as object accuracy during style unlearning.
To evaluate generation quality, we compute Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) scores across different unlearning configurations and multiplier values, quantifying both the quality and diversity of generated images. Complete results are reported for each tested model in Table <a href="#S7.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 7.7 Exploratory Variants ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and Table <a href="#S7.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 7.7 Exploratory Variants ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> in the Appendix. We also conduct specialized concept separation analysis by examining latent overlap between different concepts, with particular focus on the challenging <em id="S4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Dogs vs. Cats</em> classification case, a known limitation of current SAE-based unlearning approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> that leads to concept interference, Results are presented in Appendix <a href="#S7.SS8" title="7.8 Concept Interference Mitigation ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our robustness evaluation also includes systematic analysis of model performance across different uniform multiplier values, providing insights into stability and tunability across various unlearning intensities. We demonstrate incremental unlearning capability through sequential object removal experiments, showing that our method can handle dynamic unlearning scenarios without catastrophic interference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For adversarial robustness, we follow Zhang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> and evaluate against UnlearnDiffAtk attacks, optimizing 5-token adversarial prefixes for 40 iterations with learning rate 0.01 to provide fair comparison with baseline SAE methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Additionally, we provide UA-IRA confusion matrices for intuitive visualization of trade-offs between unlearning effectiveness and retention accuracy across different experimental conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our supervised SAE training approach across multiple model variants, exploring both training from scratch and fine-tuning existing checkpoints. Among the several tested configurations, we present the detailed analysis of <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> as our top performer. Additional results for tested variants are available in the Appendix <a href="#S7.SS7" title="7.7 Exploratory Variants ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Concept-Latent Association Distribution.</span>
Our core contribution is creating interpretable one-to-one concept-neuron mappings. Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5 Results ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is an example of the effectiveness of our supervised training in promoting feature centralization by comparing feature importance score distributions before and after training for the <em id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Flowers</em> concept. Additional examples of score distributions are reported in the Appendix <a href="#S7.SS3" title="7.3 Additional Scores Histograms ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a>. The higher score values are also tightly linked to the lower absolute values of the optimal unlearning multipliers (a deeper analysis can be found in Appendix <a href="#S7.SS2" title="7.2 Multipliers Comparison ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.21379v1/flowers_saeur_scores.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:336/161;" width="336" height="161"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.21379v1/flowers_saemnesia_scores.svg" id="S5.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:336/161;" width="336" height="161"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Feature importance score distributions for <em id="S5.F3.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Flowers</em> concept. (Left) SAeUron shows dispersed, low-magnitude scores across all neurons with maximum score of 0.0166. (Right) <span id="S5.F3.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span>shows a clear dominant peak at neuron 11979 with maximum score of 0.0404, demonstrating enhanced feature centralization with 2.43<math id="S5.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> higher score than the original model.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The original SAeUron model exhibits relatively uniform, low-magnitude scores across the entire latent space (max score: 0.015), indicating distributed concept representation. Our supervised <span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> model produces a clear dominant peak at neuron 11979 with a maximum score of 0.04, 2.67 times higher than the baseline. This concentrated activation pattern confirms that supervised training successfully enforces strong one-to-one concept-latent relationships, with the assigned neuron becoming highly specialized for the target concept while other neurons remain largely inactive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This transformation from distributed to concentrated representations is the foundation that enables all subsequent improvements in computational efficiency and unlearning performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computational Efficiency Through Interpretable Representations.</span>
The one-to-one concept-neuron mappings directly eliminate the feature combinations search complexity that affects existing methods. The methodology with unsupervised training requires exploring different numbers of top latent features for unlearning, creating a two-dimensional search space with <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times l</annotation></semantics></math> computations, where <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=7</annotation></semantics></math> possible multiplier values and <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=30</annotation></semantics></math> number of possible latent combinations required to unlearn a concept. This results in precisely 210 evaluations. In contrast, our approach requires only <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> computations since we only search multiplier values, as each concept maps to one neuron. This means exactly 7 evaluations are needed. This represents a 96.67% reduction in computational cost, achieved through interpretable concept localization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UnlearnCanvas Benchmark Performance.</span>
Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5 Results ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents our performance compared to the SAeUron baseline. An inconsistency in the computation of the metrics was discovered in the public implementation of SAeUron. We therefore recomputed the metrics ourselves using the corrected procedure.
<span id="S5.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> achieves 91.51% average score with hyperparameter search, improving over the baseline’s 82.29%. Standard deviations are reported in Appendix <a href="#S7.SS1" title="7.1 Standard deviations ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Evaluation metrics (%) of <span id="S5.T1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> against state-of-the-art methods on object concept unlearning using the UnlearnCanvas benchmark. The best result for each metric is highlighted in bold. <span id="S5.T1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> achieves superior performance across all evaluation metrics with 91.51% average score, representing an 9.22% improvement over the previous state-of-the-art.</figcaption>
