<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The performance gains stem from the stronger concept-latent associations that enable more targeted interventions. Therefore, <span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> is particularly effective for maintaining concept separation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of Uniform Multipliers.</span>
Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5 Results ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> compares the effect of applying uniform multipliers (so the same multiplier for all objects) to the latent steering for SAeUron and <span id="S5.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span>.
Across all evaluation metrics, <span id="S5.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> consistently achieves higher and more stable scores over the full range of multipliers.
This demonstrates that <span id="S5.p8.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> is less sensitive to the exact steering strength across different objects and provides more robust control of concept removal.
Additional quantitative plots are included in the Appendix <a href="#S7.SS9" title="7.9 Uniform Multipliers Analysis ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.21379v1/uniform_multipliers_finetuned.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/194;" width="685" height="194"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of uniform multipliers on unlearning performance for SAeUron and <span id="S5.F4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span>.
<span id="S5.F4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> maintains higher and more stable scores across all multipliers compared to SAeUron, indicating greater robustness to the steering strength.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qualitatively Results.</span>
Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5 Results ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows qualitative examples of <span id="S5.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> applied to a representative subset of object–style combinations.
Thanks to the one–to–one mapping between concepts and latent units, steering the sparse autoencoder selectively removes the targeted concept: the removed concepts vanish in the diagonal images, while remaining visible in the off–diagonal ones.
For highly entangled concepts such as <em id="S5.p9.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Human</em>, some residual shapes or textures may still appear after removal, reflecting the greater difficulty of isolating and suppressing such features.
Additional qualitative examples covering a wider range of objects and styles are provided in Appendix <a href="#S7.SS4" title="7.4 Additional unlearning visualization ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.21379v1/saemnesia_qualitative_results_5x5.png" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/547;" width="685" height="547" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qualitative examples of concept removal with <span id="S5.F5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span>. Each row shows a different style, and each column a different object concept.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sequential Unlearning Scalability.</span>
To demonstrate how interpretable representations enable scalability, we test sequential unlearning across 9 objects: <em id="S5.p10.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Bears</em> <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S5.p10.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Bears</em>+<em id="S5.p10.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cats</em> <math id="S5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> … <math id="S5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> 9 total objects. This challenging scenario tests whether one-to-one mappings maintain effectiveness as complexity increases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5 Results ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that <span id="S5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> significantly outperforms the baseline. <span id="S5.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> achieving 92.4% accuracy for 9-object removal compared to the baseline’s 64%. This scalability directly results from having interpretable, specialized neurons rather than distributed representations that interfere with each other.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.T2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Evaluation of <span id="S5.T2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> variants against SAeUron baseline on sequential object unlearning tasks. The best result for each task is highlighted in bold. <span id="S5.T2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> 92.4% accuracy on 9-object removal compared to SAeUron’s 64%, representing a 28.4% improvement.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S5.T2.7" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T2.7.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.7.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T2.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.7.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T2.7.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SAeUron <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite></span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.7.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T2.7.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span></th></tr>
</thead>
