<td id="S5.T2.7.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r">0.64</td>
<td id="S5.T2.7.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r"><span id="S5.T2.7.10.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.924</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p">However, this comes with retention accuracy trade-offs (Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5 Results ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). Importantly, all models maintain their operational ranges throughout the sequential unlearning process. <span id="S5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> demonstrates improved retention performance, achieving 60.9% final retention compared to the baseline’s 48.4%.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Sequential Unlearning - Retention Accuracy Comparison</span></figcaption>
<table id="S5.T3.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T3.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T3.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></th>
<th id="S5.T3.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T3.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RA Range</span></th>
<th id="S5.T3.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T3.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Final RA</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T3.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">SAeUron <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite></td>
<td id="S5.T3.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">40-48%</td>
<td id="S5.T3.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">48.4%</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r"><span id="S5.T3.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_r">46-61%</td>
<td id="S5.T3.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_r">60.9%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adversarial Robustness.</span> The concentrated concept representations also improve adversarial robustness, as shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5 Results ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Under UnlearnDiffAtk attacks, <span id="S5.p13.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> demonstrates improved resilience, dropping from 73.30% to 28.30% unlearning accuracy. This contrasts sharply with the state-of-the-art significant drop from 26.19% to 2.22%. The supervised concept-neuron mapping approach in <span id="S5.p13.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> shows substantially improved robustness against adversarial perturbations, with the stronger concept-neuron bonds making it significantly harder for attacks to disrupt the specialized representations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.21379v1/unlearning_accuracy_comparison.svg" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/294;" width="685" height="294"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F6.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S5.F6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Unlearning accuracy before and after UnlearnDiffAtk adversarial attacks.<span id="S5.F6.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <span id="S5.F6.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> maintains higher performance under adversarial attack compared to the SAeUron baseline. <span id="S5.F6.7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> drops from 73.30% to 28.30%, compared to SAeUron’s drop from 26.19% to 2.22%.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusions and Limitations</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span>, a supervised SAE training approach for concept unlearning in diffusion models. Our method mitigates feature splitting and enhances feature centralization, straightening one-to-one concept-latent mappings through automatic concept labeling and direct supervision during training, demonstrating improvements in unlearning effectiveness while maintaining competitive generation quality. Our approach achieves better concept-latent associations compared to unsupervised baselines, reaching 91.51% average performance on the UnlearnCanvas benchmark. Additionally, our methodology reduces hyperparameter search by 96.67% with respect to current approaches, while on sequential unlearning tasks, it demonstrates superior scalability with a 28.4% improvement in unlearning accuracy for 9-object removal. However, we acknowledge that <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> has some limitations that restrict the generalizability and practical deployment of our approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Scope.</span> Our evaluation focuses primarily on the UnlearnCanvas benchmark, following the evaluation protocol established in the literature of SAE-based unlearning, to ensure fair comparison. While we employ multiple complementary metrics including unlearning accuracy, retention accuracy across domains, FID scores, and adversarial robustness measures, validation on additional benchmarks with different concept distributions and evaluation protocols would strengthen the generalizability of our findings. Future work could expand evaluation to other concept unlearning benchmarks as they become available in this emerging field.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computational Trade-offs.</span> Our approach achieves significant computational savings by eliminating the need for percentile-based feature threshold search, which typically requires exploring 1-30+ features per concept in baseline methods. Instead, our supervised training enforces fixed one-neuron-per-concept assignments, substantially reducing the hyperparameter search space. However, our method still requires search over multiplier values, reported in Table <a href="#S7.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 7.2 Multipliers Comparison ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diffusion Models Architectures.</span> Our experiments focus on SD1.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, following the experimental setup established in the SAE-based unlearning literature, to enable direct performance comparison. While this choice ensures fair evaluation against the state-of-the-art, it limits generalization across the broader landscape of text-to-image generation models. Different diffusion architectures may exhibit varying internal representations, cross-attention mechanisms, and concept encoding strategies that could influence the effectiveness of SAE approaches. Similarly, our focus on specific cross-attention blocks (<span id="S6.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">up.1.1</span>) reflects established practice for concept manipulation in SD1.5, though the optimal intervention points may differ across model architectures. Future work will explore the transferability of our supervised training approach to newer model generations and alternative diffusion architectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ethical Considerations.</span> While our method aims to remove harmful content generation capabilities, concept unlearning techniques could potentially be misused for censorship or may inadvertently encode biases through automatic concept labeling. Due to adversarial attacks, unlearning interventions can be circumvented through carefully crafted prompts, requiring ongoing robustness analysis for safe deployment.


<span id="S6.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
</section>
