<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Multipliers Comparison</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The comparison reveals significant differences in unlearning strategies between the baseline method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and our <span id="S7.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> variants. The baseline method employs substantially larger multiplier magnitudes (as per Table <a href="#S7.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 7.2 Multipliers Comparison ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), averaging -21.25, while our <span id="S7.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span>-OS-CA-FS and <span id="S7.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span>-OS-OC-CA-FT models achieve effective unlearning with more conservative multipliers, averaging -6.20 and -6.60 respectively.
These results align well with the findings on latents score distributions shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5 Results ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, Figure <a href="#S7.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 7.3 Additional Scores Histograms ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, Figure <a href="#S7.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 7.3 Additional Scores Histograms ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and Figure <a href="#S7.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 7.3 Additional Scores Histograms ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.
This substantial reduction in multiplier magnitude supports the hypothesis that feature centralization allows effective unlearning by focusing on individual high-scoring features.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S7.T5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 5</span>: </span><span id="S7.T5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of Object Unlearning Multipliers Across Three Models</span></figcaption>
<table id="S7.T5.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
