<section id="S7.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.5 </span>Global Cross-Entropy</h3>

<div id="S7.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ Cross-entropy (CE) loss with softmax applied to all pre-TopK latent activations as an alternative to the Concept-Assignment Loss (Equation <a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). This way, we treat concept assignment as a classification problem across the entire latent space:</p>
<table id="S7.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{global-ce}}=-\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\log\left(\frac{\exp(v^{i}_{c_{i}})}{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\exp(v^{i}_{j})}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>global-ce</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{global-ce}}=-\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\log\left(\frac{\exp(v^{i}_{c_{i}})}{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\exp(v^{i}_{j})}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the batch size, <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the pre-TopK activation value for sample <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> at latent position <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the assigned concept latent index for sample <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of latents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This approach, however, led to suboptimal performance as reported in Table <a href="#S7.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 7.7 Exploratory Variants ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and Table <a href="#S7.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 7.7 Exploratory Variants ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> w.r.t. OO-GCE-FS and OO-GCE-FT.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.6 </span>Post-TopK Loss Analysis</h3>

<div id="S7.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">OS-TK-CA-FT is the only model variant that applies CA loss after the Top-K, yet demonstrates competitive performance without major degradation. In fine-tuned configurations with hyperparameter search (Table <a href="#S7.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 7.7 Exploratory Variants ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), OS-TK-CA-FT achieves 85.52% average performance compared to the baseline’s 82.29%, representing a meaningful 3.2% improvement. Similarly, in from-scratch training (Table <a href="#S7.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 7.7 Exploratory Variants ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), OS-TK-CA-FT maintains 86.93% performance, indicating that post-TopK supervision remains viable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.7 </span>Exploratory Variants</h3>

<div id="S7.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#S7.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 7.7 Exploratory Variants ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, all the variants tested for this work are presented.
SAEmnesia-OS-OC-CA-FT is the version reported as <span id="S7.SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> in the main paper.</p>
</div>
