<section id="S7.SS9" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.9 </span>Uniform Multipliers Analysis</h3>

<div id="S7.SS9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The uniform multiplier sweep analysis (Figure <a href="#S7.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ 7.9 Uniform Multipliers Analysis ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> and Figure <a href="#S7.F15" title="Figure 15 ‣ 7.9 Uniform Multipliers Analysis ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) reveals varying performance characteristics across model versions and evaluation metrics. In the fine-tuned models (Figure <a href="#S7.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ 7.9 Uniform Multipliers Analysis ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), <span id="S7.SS9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> variants show competitive performance with some variations across different multiplier ranges, with certain variants like S-OS-OC-CA-FT maintaining strong performance while others such as S-OS-TK-CA-FT exhibit sensitivity to specific multiplier settings. The from-scratch models (Figure <a href="#S7.F15" title="Figure 15 ‣ 7.9 Uniform Multipliers Analysis ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) demonstrate different behavior patterns, with variants like S-OO-CA-FS showing particular sensitivity to moderate multiplier values before recovering at gentler settings. Both figures indicate that <span id="S7.SS9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> variants achieve reasonable performance across various hyperparameter ranges. The results suggest that different <span id="S7.SS9.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> configurations may be better suited for different multiplier ranges, highlighting the importance of hyperparameter selection in optimizing unlearning performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F14" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.21379v1/x1.svg" id="S7.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/164;" width="685" height="164"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F14.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 14</span>: </span><span id="S7.F14.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">All Model’s performances with uniform multipliers - fine tuned.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S7.F15" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.21379v1/uniform_multipliers_from_scratch.svg" id="S7.F15.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/164;" width="685" height="164"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F15.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 15</span>: </span><span id="S7.F15.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">All Model’s performances with uniform multipliers - from scratch.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS10" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.10 </span>Lambda Coefficient Analysis for <span id="S7.SS10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SAEmnesia</span> Loss</h3>

<div id="S7.SS10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The choice of value for <math id="S7.SS10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation <a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> as the coefficient for the <math id="S7.SS10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{supSAE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{supSAE}</annotation></semantics></math> loss critically affects model performance. Our best experimental results were achieved with <math id="S7.SS10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=3</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S7.SS10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=10</annotation></semantics></math>, the concept assignment loss becomes too aggressive, causing the model to produce completely white images and lose generation capabilities. In our experimental setup, <math id="S7.SS10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> from Equation <a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> was set to <math id="S7.SS10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> from Equation <a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ SAEmnesia: Erasing Concepts in Diffusion Models with Sparse Autoencoders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> was set to <math id="S7.SS10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
